Annotation-based Event Listeners :-
You can register an event listener on any method of a managed bean by using the @EventListener
annotation.
public class BlockedListNotifier {
  private String notificationAddress;
  public void setNotificationAddress(String notificationAddress) {
  this.notificationAddress = notificationAddress;
  }
//annotation used 
  @EventListener
  public void processBlockedListEvent(BlockedListEvent event) {
  // notify appropriate parties via notificationAddress...
  }
}

Benefit :- 
Flexible name and without implements to specific listener interface.
Event type can also be narrowred through generics.

Registering an Event Listener with @EventListener
You can annotate any method in a Spring bean with @EventListener to make it listen for a specific event.
The event type is determined from the method's parameter.
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class BlockedListNotifier {

    private String notificationAddress;

    public void setNotificationAddress(String notificationAddress) {
        this.notificationAddress = notificationAddress;
    }

    @EventListener
    public void processBlockedListEvent(BlockedListEvent event) {
        // Notify appropriate parties using notificationAddress...
        System.out.println("BlockedListEvent received. Notifying: " + notificationAddress);
    }
}
The method listens for BlockedListEvent.
No need to implement ApplicationListener manually.

Listening to Multiple Events
If a method should listen to multiple event types, specify them inside @EventListener.

Example: Listening to Multiple Events

@EventListener({ContextStartedEvent.class, ContextRefreshedEvent.class})
public void handleContextEvents() {
    System.out.println("ContextStartedEvent or ContextRefreshedEvent occurred");
}

Conditional Event Handling with SpEL
You can filter events dynamically using Spring Expression Language (SpEL) inside 
@EventListener.
Example: Process Event Only If content Equals "my-event"
@EventListener(condition = "#blEvent.content == 'my-event'")
public void processBlockedListEvent(BlockedListEvent blEvent) {
    System.out.println("Processing blocked list event: " + blEvent.getContent());
}

SpEL Context Variables Available:

Name	             Description	            Example
#root.event	       Access the event object	#root.event
#args	Arguments    passed to the method	    #args[0] (first argument)
#p0 / #a0	First    argument alias	           #p0.content == 'my-event'

Publishing an Event from Another Event Listener
An event listener method can return another event, which Spring will automatically 
publish.

Example: Forwarding an Event
@EventListener
public ListUpdateEvent handleBlockedListEvent(BlockedListEvent event) {
    System.out.println("Handling BlockedListEvent and publishing ListUpdateEvent");
    return new ListUpdateEvent(event.getSource(), "List Updated");
}
The method listens to BlockedListEvent and publishes a ListUpdateEvent.

If multiple events need to be published, return a Collection or an array of events.

Asynchronous Listner :-
If you want a particular listener to process events asynchronously, you can reuse the regular @Async
support.
@EventListener
@Async
public void processBlockedListEvent(BlockedListEvent event) {
  // BlockedListEvent is processed in a separate thread
}

Handling Exceptions in Asynchronous Event Listeners
In Spring, event listeners can be executed asynchronously by using the @Async annotation. However, when an asynchronous event listener throws an exception:

The exception is not propagated back to the caller.

The caller continues execution without being blocked by the exception.

Spring provides AsyncUncaughtExceptionHandler to handle uncaught exceptions.

Additionally, asynchronous event listener methods cannot return a value to publish another event. 
Instead, an ApplicationEventPublisher must 
be used to manually publish events.
